d(150,13,'* Giftig næring for en alt for følsom sjæl, du, uden hvem jeg aldrig kunne kende lykken! Ømme tungsind, kom og trøst mig, stil kvalen i min mørke ensomhed, og bland en hemmelig sødme i de tårer, jeg mærker rinde.')
e(116,6,'Got up late. When I woke I lay a long time in bed, giving in to laziness. O God, help me and strengthen me to walk in Your ways! Read the Scriptures, but without proper feeling. Brother Urusov came. We talked about the vanities of the world. He told me about the Emperor’s new projects. I began to criticize them but recalled my rules and my benefactor’s words: a true Freemason works zealously for the state when his help is needed and looks quietly on when nobody asks it of him. My tongue is my enemy. Brothers G. V. and O. called, and we had a first discussion about admitting a new Brother. They gave me the office of Rhetor. I feel weak and unworthy. We spoke of the meaning of the Temple’s seven pillars and steps, the seven sciences, the seven virtues, the seven vices and the seven gifts of the Holy Spirit. Brother O. spoke very well. The admission took place this evening. The new decorations in the lodge greatly added to the splendor of the ceremony. It was Boris Drubetskoy we admitted. I nominated him and acted as Rhetor. All the time I stood alone with him in the dark chamber I felt strangely agitated. I caught myself hating him and tried in vain to overcome it. For that very reason I long to save him from evil and set him on the path of truth, but the evil thoughts about him persisted. I felt he had joined the Brotherhood simply to make friends and gain favor with members of our lodge. He had asked me several times whether N. and S. were members, a question I could not answer. To my eyes he is incapable of respect for our holy order and far too taken up and pleased with the outward man to want to improve his soul. Apart from that I had no cause to doubt him. Yet he struck me as insincere. All the time we stood alone in the dark temple I fancied he was smiling contemptuously at my words, and I truly wanted to plunge into his bare breast the sword I held against it. I could neither speak with eloquence nor tell the Brothers and the Grand Master frankly of my doubts. Great Architect of Nature, help me find the true path out of this labyrinth of lies!')
e(334,22,modern(334,22)+' From her look, her exclamations and her brief questions Pierre could tell she understood precisely what he wanted to convey. She could follow not only what he said but what he longed to say and could find no words for. This was his account of the child and of the woman he had been arrested for protecting: “It was awful. Abandoned children, some of them in the flames … They snatched one out right in front of me … and women having their things torn off them, their earrings ripped out …” He flushed and grew confused. “Then a patrol came up and they arrested all the men—all the men who weren’t looting, I mean. I was among them.”')
